WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.033 --> 00:00:00.834
The FAA is

00:00:00.834 --> 00:00:02.102
now investigating

00:00:02.102 --> 00:00:04.070
a near collision midair

00:00:04.070 --> 00:00:05.238
between two planes.

00:00:05.238 --> 00:00:06.439
Sun this over Fort

00:00:06.439 --> 00:00:07.507
Lauderdale, Florida,

00:00:07.507 --> 00:00:09.476
pilot of Allegiant Flight

00:00:09.476 --> 00:00:10.410
four 85

00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:11.945
bound for Lexington, Kentucky,

00:00:11.945 --> 00:00:14.214
was forced to take evasive action

00:00:14.214 --> 00:00:15.315
after nearly hitting

00:00:15.315 --> 00:00:17.117
a Gulfstream business jet.

00:00:17.117 --> 00:00:19.185
A flight attendant on that Allegiant jet

00:00:19.652 --> 00:00:20.887
was hurt.

00:00:20.887 --> 00:00:23.523
Passenger Erika Zachary, seen here

00:00:23.523 --> 00:00:24.424
on the flight, said

00:00:24.424 --> 00:00:26.760
it felt like the plane went straight up.

00:00:27.127 --> 00:00:28.261
She was visibly upset

00:00:28.261 --> 00:00:29.763
after seeing the injured attendant

00:00:29.763 --> 00:00:32.098
who Thacker says fell backward

00:00:32.432 --> 00:00:33.733
as the plane ascended.

00:00:33.733 --> 00:00:34.768
CNN correspondent Gabe

00:00:34.768 --> 00:00:36.569
Cohen joins me now.

00:00:36.569 --> 00:00:37.937
Gabe, first

00:00:37.937 --> 00:00:38.905
tell me how these jets

00:00:38.905 --> 00:00:40.040
got so close together.

00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:41.174
There's so many systems,

00:00:41.174 --> 00:00:42.809
failsafe, failsafe,

00:00:42.809 --> 00:00:44.477
some automatic humans

00:00:44.477 --> 00:00:46.513
involved in air traffic control.

00:00:46.513 --> 00:00:47.480
How did this happen?

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:48.648
Yeah, and that's the big question

00:00:48.648 --> 00:00:49.382
that the FAA

00:00:49.382 --> 00:00:50.884
is investigating at this point.

00:00:50.884 --> 00:00:51.684
Let me lay out, though,

00:00:51.684 --> 00:00:52.886
exactly how this played out,

00:00:52.886 --> 00:00:53.887
what we know at this point.

00:00:53.887 --> 00:00:54.921
So, Allegiant,

00:00:54.921 --> 00:00:56.756
this flight takes off from Fort

00:00:56.756 --> 00:00:58.725
Lauderdale on Sunday

00:00:58.725 --> 00:01:00.126
headed for Kentucky,

00:01:00.126 --> 00:01:02.128
right around the Orlando area.

00:01:02.162 --> 00:01:04.197
It's about 23,000 feet up

00:01:04.330 --> 00:01:05.999
when suddenly the pilot

00:01:05.999 --> 00:01:07.667
is told to go east.

00:01:07.667 --> 00:01:09.135
We have this statement

00:01:09.135 --> 00:01:11.371
from the FAA explaining what happened.

00:01:11.371 --> 00:01:12.138
They basically said

00:01:12.138 --> 00:01:13.573
that air traffic control

00:01:13.573 --> 00:01:15.608
in Miami directed the pilot

00:01:15.608 --> 00:01:17.310
to turn eastbound over

00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:18.144
the ocean

00:01:18.144 --> 00:01:21.181
at that 23,000 feet a foot altitude

00:01:21.347 --> 00:01:24.350
when it crossed in front of a northbound

00:01:24.350 --> 00:01:25.885
Gulfstream business jet.

00:01:25.885 --> 00:01:26.619
So at that point,

00:01:26.619 --> 00:01:27.787
these two aircraft

00:01:27.787 --> 00:01:29.489
are headed in the same path.

00:01:29.489 --> 00:01:30.557
But both pilots

00:01:30.557 --> 00:01:33.626
fortunately got an automated alert

00:01:33.626 --> 00:01:35.462
that basically there was another plane

00:01:35.462 --> 00:01:36.996
flying at the same altitude.

00:01:36.996 --> 00:01:39.065
An immediate action needed to be taken.

00:01:39.299 --> 00:01:39.799
Bear in mind,

00:01:39.799 --> 00:01:40.500
they were about two

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:41.935
and a half miles apart.

00:01:41.935 --> 00:01:43.503
That may sound like a big distance,

00:01:43.503 --> 00:01:44.471
but this yeah,

00:01:44.471 --> 00:01:46.439
this is airborne highway traffic speeds.

00:01:46.439 --> 00:01:47.674
We're talking about seconds

00:01:47.674 --> 00:01:48.541
not minutes here.

00:01:48.541 --> 00:01:49.943
Both of those pilots, though,

00:01:49.943 --> 00:01:51.344
took those evasive actions.

00:01:51.344 --> 00:01:53.279
The Allegiant plane went up,

00:01:53.279 --> 00:01:55.381
the jet went down,

00:01:55.381 --> 00:01:57.851
and they were able to avoid the disaster

00:01:57.851 --> 00:01:59.452
but we know that the Allegiance maneuver,

00:01:59.452 --> 00:01:59.953
that pilot's

00:01:59.953 --> 00:02:01.821
maneuver was dramatic enough

00:02:01.821 --> 00:02:03.123
that it injured at least one

00:02:03.123 --> 00:02:04.290
flight attendant on board.

00:02:04.290 --> 00:02:06.893
They had to turn back to Fort Lauderdale.

00:02:06.893 --> 00:02:07.560
So that person

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:08.761
could get medical attention.

00:02:08.761 --> 00:02:09.963
And remember,

00:02:09.963 --> 00:02:10.830
it's rare

00:02:10.830 --> 00:02:12.532
that an incident like this, a pilot's

00:02:12.532 --> 00:02:13.867
maneuver, ends in an injury.

00:02:13.867 --> 00:02:14.801
We sometimes hear about it

00:02:14.801 --> 00:02:17.103
with turbulence, but this is really rare.

00:02:17.137 --> 00:02:18.571
I mean, they had to move quickly here.

00:02:18.571 --> 00:02:20.940
So anecdotally,

00:02:21.174 --> 00:02:23.176
it seems like we have seen

00:02:23.176 --> 00:02:24.210
a remarkable number

00:02:24.210 --> 00:02:24.644
of these

00:02:24.644 --> 00:02:25.445
in recent months,

00:02:25.445 --> 00:02:26.312
both in the air

00:02:26.312 --> 00:02:28.548
and on the ground, statistically.

00:02:28.882 --> 00:02:30.416
Are these near-misses happening

00:02:30.416 --> 00:02:31.050
more often?

00:02:31.050 --> 00:02:32.352
Yeah, well, that's been the conversation

00:02:32.352 --> 00:02:32.852
throughout the year.

00:02:32.852 --> 00:02:33.887
And we have heard people

00:02:33.887 --> 00:02:34.954
like transportation

00:02:34.954 --> 00:02:36.422
secretary people to

00:02:36.422 --> 00:02:37.757
talk about early in the year,

00:02:37.757 --> 00:02:39.392
a surge in close

00:02:39.392 --> 00:02:41.494
calls, especially incursions

00:02:41.494 --> 00:02:42.729
involving at least one plane

00:02:42.729 --> 00:02:44.464
that was sitting on a runway.

00:02:44.464 --> 00:02:45.932
And we know the FAA has faced

00:02:45.932 --> 00:02:48.234
intense scrutiny, claims

00:02:48.234 --> 00:02:49.936
that air traffic control is understaffed,

00:02:49.936 --> 00:02:51.037
using outdated systems.

00:02:51.037 --> 00:02:52.672
And they have taken

00:02:52.672 --> 00:02:54.274
precautions, taken measures

00:02:54.274 --> 00:02:55.008
to address this.

00:02:55.008 --> 00:02:56.142
They held that safety summit.

00:02:56.142 --> 00:02:57.810
You might remember earlier in the year.

00:02:57.810 --> 00:02:58.978
They added new guidelines,

00:02:58.978 --> 00:03:00.780
guidelines for air traffic control

00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:02.482
and stood up a new

00:03:02.482 --> 00:03:04.284
regular training program

00:03:04.284 --> 00:03:05.084
to try to make sure

00:03:05.084 --> 00:03:06.553
that air traffic controllers

00:03:06.553 --> 00:03:07.253
stay sharp,

00:03:07.253 --> 00:03:08.555
that they don't make mistakes, that

00:03:08.555 --> 00:03:09.822
we don't have close calls

00:03:09.822 --> 00:03:11.191
like the incident we just saw.

00:03:11.191 --> 00:03:13.626
But but remember, as much as experts

00:03:13.626 --> 00:03:14.227
and officials

00:03:14.227 --> 00:03:16.262
in this field tout the safety measures

00:03:16.429 --> 00:03:18.097
and the safety track record

00:03:18.097 --> 00:03:20.033
that's in place for the airline industry.

00:03:20.033 --> 00:03:21.367
We're talking about an FAA

00:03:21.367 --> 00:03:22.335
that has not had

00:03:22.335 --> 00:03:23.970
a confirmed administrator

00:03:23.970 --> 00:03:25.705
for more than a year now.

00:03:25.705 --> 00:03:27.840
President Biden tried to appoint

00:03:28.808 --> 00:03:31.177
a administrator

00:03:31.177 --> 00:03:33.580
that was then resigned under scrutiny.

00:03:33.580 --> 00:03:34.948
And so there are a lot of questions

00:03:34.948 --> 00:03:37.217
when you look at an incident like this

00:03:37.217 --> 00:03:39.252
as to whether some of the changes

00:03:39.252 --> 00:03:40.620
they've put in place are working.

00:03:40.620 --> 00:03:42.088
How long can they keep

00:03:42.088 --> 00:03:43.289
that really,

00:03:43.289 --> 00:03:45.024
really good track record pace

00:03:45.024 --> 00:03:45.592
We've also heard

00:03:45.592 --> 00:03:46.793
the transportation secretary

00:03:46.793 --> 00:03:47.860
talk about how many more

00:03:47.860 --> 00:03:49.095
air traffic controllers

00:03:49.095 --> 00:03:50.330
they want to need to hire.

00:03:50.330 --> 00:03:52.365
Gabe Cohen, thanks so much.

00:03:52.365 --> 00:03:53.633
So what was it

00:03:53.633 --> 00:03:56.069
like on that jet as this happened?

00:03:56.102 --> 00:03:57.136
Joining me now,

00:03:57.136 --> 00:03:58.338
Erica Thacker,

00:03:58.338 --> 00:03:59.339
who, as we mentioned

00:03:59.339 --> 00:04:01.507
earlier, was on board.

00:04:01.507 --> 00:04:03.710
Jerrica, thanks so much for joining.

00:04:03.710 --> 00:04:05.011
I'm glad you're okay.

00:04:05.011 --> 00:04:05.979
I'm glad other folks on

00:04:05.979 --> 00:04:08.114
that flight are okay.

00:04:08.114 --> 00:04:11.384
First, tell us what it was like as

00:04:11.384 --> 00:04:14.487
a pilot had to make this residency move

00:04:14.988 --> 00:04:16.589
out of the way of this other jet

00:04:19.025 --> 00:04:20.393
Well, at that time,

00:04:20.393 --> 00:04:21.794
we thought it was turbulence,

00:04:21.794 --> 00:04:23.630
which was still scary at the time

00:04:23.630 --> 00:04:25.765
because I had never been through that.

00:04:25.765 --> 00:04:27.300
But when he came on the intercom

00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:29.802
and let us know that we were actually

00:04:30.436 --> 00:04:32.772
trying to miss the other airplane,

00:04:32.772 --> 00:04:34.073
it was like an intensified

00:04:34.073 --> 00:04:36.009
all that feared that happened.

00:04:36.009 --> 00:04:37.677
And it was like a roller coaster

00:04:37.677 --> 00:04:39.145
the way that we had to go up

00:04:39.145 --> 00:04:40.480
and down to miss the plane

00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:41.681
and then level back out to the

00:04:41.681 --> 00:04:43.349
what we were supposed to be at.

00:04:43.349 --> 00:04:43.850
Goodness.

00:04:43.850 --> 00:04:46.019
What was the reaction on board

00:04:46.019 --> 00:04:47.220
from yourself and from others

00:04:47.220 --> 00:04:49.122
when you heard the pilot

00:04:49.122 --> 00:04:51.391
say, that's what I had to do.

00:04:51.424 --> 00:04:54.127
I had to steer us out of the way

00:04:55.061 --> 00:04:56.729
People started crying.

00:04:56.729 --> 00:04:58.398
They hear people praying.

00:04:58.398 --> 00:05:00.333
And everyone who was at a window seat

00:05:00.333 --> 00:05:02.068
started to close their windows

00:05:02.068 --> 00:05:03.336
because everybody was too scared

00:05:03.336 --> 00:05:05.705
to look outside of what was around us.

00:05:05.705 --> 00:05:06.506
Goodness.

00:05:06.506 --> 00:05:09.208
Now, you say you saw the flight attendant

00:05:09.776 --> 00:05:11.744
who was thrown during this maneuver

00:05:11.744 --> 00:05:14.013
get get hurt. What did you see

00:05:16.616 --> 00:05:17.450
Well, when I

00:05:17.450 --> 00:05:18.751
after I looked over, after

00:05:18.751 --> 00:05:20.053
we had finally stable out,

00:05:20.053 --> 00:05:21.387
I saw that two fire

00:05:21.387 --> 00:05:22.922
tenants were on the ground.

00:05:22.922 --> 00:05:24.791
And when one got up, the other one

00:05:24.791 --> 00:05:26.225
stayed on the ground.

00:05:26.225 --> 00:05:28.828
And the one who got up said, oh, my God,

00:05:28.828 --> 00:05:30.129
she hit her head.

00:05:30.129 --> 00:05:31.731
So she stayed laying on the ground

00:05:31.731 --> 00:05:33.299
for about 5 minutes.

00:05:33.299 --> 00:05:34.334
When they finally were able

00:05:34.334 --> 00:05:35.034
to get her up

00:05:35.034 --> 00:05:35.401
and move

00:05:35.401 --> 00:05:36.836
her back to the back of the plane

00:05:36.836 --> 00:05:37.537
and then asked

00:05:37.537 --> 00:05:38.604
if there was any

00:05:38.604 --> 00:05:40.173
medical professionals on board,

00:05:40.173 --> 00:05:41.641
which they eventually found one

00:05:41.641 --> 00:05:43.142
and brought her back with that injured

00:05:43.142 --> 00:05:45.912
flight attendant and did it.

00:05:45.945 --> 00:05:47.580
Were they able to find a doctor on board

00:05:47.580 --> 00:05:50.350
who could take care of her immediately?

00:05:50.717 --> 00:05:51.751
There was a woman on board

00:05:51.751 --> 00:05:52.685
who went back with her.

00:05:52.685 --> 00:05:53.619
I'm not sure what they did.

00:05:53.619 --> 00:05:54.053
Back there,

00:05:54.053 --> 00:05:56.389
but she did go back to help her out.

00:05:56.389 --> 00:05:59.826
Now, as I understand it, this incredibly

00:05:59.826 --> 00:06:01.260
was only the second time

00:06:01.260 --> 00:06:02.228
you've been on a plane,

00:06:02.228 --> 00:06:03.096
your second flight

00:06:04.964 --> 00:06:05.932
Yes.

00:06:06.532 --> 00:06:08.000
What?

00:06:08.067 --> 00:06:09.435
First of all, I'm glad you're well.

00:06:09.435 --> 00:06:10.169
How are you doing now?

00:06:10.169 --> 00:06:11.704
And what are you thinking

00:06:13.573 --> 00:06:14.774
I'm doing a lot better.

00:06:14.774 --> 00:06:16.275
It's still it's not like

00:06:16.275 --> 00:06:16.943
I go through the day

00:06:16.943 --> 00:06:18.111
without in the back of my head.

00:06:18.111 --> 00:06:20.980
But when you lay down to go to bed, it's

00:06:20.980 --> 00:06:22.382
still in the back.

00:06:22.382 --> 00:06:23.750
Of your head there

00:06:23.750 --> 00:06:24.751
that the thing

00:06:24.751 --> 00:06:25.952
that you almost went through,

00:06:25.952 --> 00:06:26.652
the fact

00:06:26.652 --> 00:06:29.322
that I could have died in a few seconds

00:06:29.856 --> 00:06:31.290
and still talking about it

00:06:31.290 --> 00:06:33.326
now, I just now got to the point

00:06:33.326 --> 00:06:35.461
where I could actually talk about

00:06:35.461 --> 00:06:36.462
what actually happened

00:06:36.462 --> 00:06:38.231
during the incident, avoiding the plane,

00:06:38.231 --> 00:06:39.198
because it was kind

00:06:39.198 --> 00:06:40.366
of blanked out in my mind.

00:06:40.366 --> 00:06:41.401
And I didn't realize

00:06:41.401 --> 00:06:43.102
when I was talking to people about it

00:06:43.102 --> 00:06:44.971
that I was completely skipping

00:06:44.971 --> 00:06:45.972
the actual incident.

00:06:47.507 --> 00:06:47.974
Yeah, that's

00:06:47.974 --> 00:06:48.474
listen, that's

00:06:48.474 --> 00:06:49.642
it's a it's often

00:06:49.642 --> 00:06:51.677
a typical reaction to trauma.

00:06:51.677 --> 00:06:52.712
Can I ask you,

00:06:52.712 --> 00:06:53.312
first of all,

00:06:53.312 --> 00:06:53.913
get the help

00:06:53.913 --> 00:06:55.515
you need to talk to folks about it.

00:06:55.515 --> 00:06:56.783
That's important.

00:06:56.783 --> 00:06:57.483
Do you think you'll get it

00:06:57.483 --> 00:06:59.852
back on a plane someday?

00:07:01.721 --> 00:07:03.189
There is a very slim chance

00:07:03.189 --> 00:07:05.024
that I will step onto another plane.

00:07:05.024 --> 00:07:06.259
But I can tell you

00:07:06.259 --> 00:07:08.327
if it ever does come to that point,

00:07:08.327 --> 00:07:10.630
I will not be going on Allegiant

00:07:10.630 --> 00:07:11.631
My goodness.

00:07:11.631 --> 00:07:12.865
Did they reach out to you?

00:07:12.865 --> 00:07:16.335
Apologize No.

00:07:16.335 --> 00:07:18.604
We did receive a $100 voucher

00:07:18.604 --> 00:07:20.139
for a future floor,

00:07:20.139 --> 00:07:22.642
but there was no real communication

00:07:22.642 --> 00:07:24.911
with the Legion at all.

00:07:24.944 --> 00:07:25.545
Goodness.

00:07:25.545 --> 00:07:28.481
The size of the truck.

00:07:28.481 --> 00:07:31.050
And they lied about what happened.

00:07:31.050 --> 00:07:32.985
They lied how

00:07:34.120 --> 00:07:36.222
well, my mother called once we landed

00:07:36.222 --> 00:07:38.291
because we needed help to

00:07:38.291 --> 00:07:39.292
get a rental car

00:07:39.292 --> 00:07:39.992
because we didn't have

00:07:39.992 --> 00:07:41.594
that kind of money on us.

00:07:41.594 --> 00:07:43.563
And so my mom called and was like,

00:07:43.563 --> 00:07:44.831
Is there any way you guys can help

00:07:44.831 --> 00:07:47.600
my family get a rental car to get home?

00:07:48.067 --> 00:07:49.635
Because they were on that airplane

00:07:49.635 --> 00:07:51.337
that almost hit another airplane.

00:07:51.337 --> 00:07:53.105
And Allegiant told my mom

00:07:53.105 --> 00:07:54.874
you have the wrong information.

00:07:54.874 --> 00:07:56.242
The plane was turned around

00:07:56.242 --> 00:07:58.377
because the pilot got sick

00:07:58.377 --> 00:07:59.612
and hung up on. Really?

00:08:00.580 --> 00:08:02.248
Goodness gracious.

00:08:02.248 --> 00:08:02.682
Wow.

00:08:02.682 --> 00:08:05.318
Well, that's certainly not fair to you.

00:08:05.318 --> 00:08:07.019
Listen, you know, statistically, it's

00:08:07.019 --> 00:08:08.788
still the safest way to travel.

00:08:08.788 --> 00:08:09.956
We know that.

00:08:09.956 --> 00:08:11.290
But but, boy, having gone

00:08:11.290 --> 00:08:13.226
what you go through, I just hope

00:08:13.226 --> 00:08:14.360
I hope you get over it soon.

00:08:14.360 --> 00:08:16.929
And I'm glad you're safe now.

00:08:17.697 --> 00:08:18.064
Thank you.

00:08:18.064 --> 00:08:19.265
I appreciate it.

00:08:19.532 --> 00:08:20.666
Thanks so much for joining us,

00:08:20.666 --> 00:08:21.300
Erica Thacker.

